WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.010
Hello and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today we're

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.680
going to be specifically looking
at alkanes and how to name them

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.929
whenever they have different isomers
of propyl and butyls as substituents.

00:00:14.439 --> 00:00:18.399
So one of the big things about these
different groups is that with propyl

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.080
and butyl is that they start adding
a lot more carbons, meaning that

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.580
eventually they're not always going to
be just in a straight chain anymore.

00:00:25.895 --> 00:00:28.335
We're going to start seeing
some different shapes arise.

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:32.775
So the first one is, let's kind of
just focus on just propyl, right?

00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:37.124
Propyl if you remember is three carbons,
so therefore if you have like let's say

00:00:37.125 --> 00:00:42.964
a chain like this one here, so if I were
to let's say add a propyl onto it, well

00:00:42.964 --> 00:00:47.375
the big thing is whenever you start kind
of adding these bigger chains is that

00:00:47.385 --> 00:00:51.685
there's kind of a limit to where you
can realistically place them, um, just

00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:55.645
because if you sort of place them like
too close to the ends, it's going to end

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.705
up redefining your longest parent chain.

00:00:58.965 --> 00:01:02.445
So typically you only ever have them
in the middle and they tend to be

00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:05.964
even a little bit more rarer and
especially in terms of organic chemistry

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.945
one and introductory chemistry.

00:01:08.444 --> 00:01:11.914
So if I kind of have this in the middle
here, um, and I wanted to name it.

00:01:11.915 --> 00:01:14.364
So if I kind of named it from
the left and I say one, two,

00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:18.915
three, four, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:21.215
Well, how would I end up naming this?

00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:26.345
Well, if you remember from my previous
lessons or from my other videos, that

00:01:26.345 --> 00:01:33.635
this would be 5 methyl, or sorry, not 5
methyl, 5 propyl nonane would be the name.

00:01:33.974 --> 00:01:36.054
Now, is this the only shape?

00:01:36.105 --> 00:01:40.445
Well, there's other shapes possible,
and it has to do with isomers.

00:01:40.884 --> 00:01:45.735
So if you remember, isomers are whenever
you have the same exact chemical formula.

00:01:46.244 --> 00:01:48.404
Um, but it has a different structure.

00:01:48.404 --> 00:01:50.574
So propyl has one of them.

00:01:50.995 --> 00:01:55.474
So essentially for propyl, it can
actually form this Y shape that is right

00:01:55.485 --> 00:01:56.755
next to me where it's isosymmetric.

00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:03.334
So you can see that when you look
at this, uh, structure here that the

00:02:03.404 --> 00:02:06.805
R, remember that's the rest of the
molecule, so that's referring to your

00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:13.170
chain, It's connected to a single carbon
and then it's split on both sides.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.710
And you can see that too in the skeletal
structure as well that it has that split.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.009
So essentially if you ever were to put
this in a structure it allows it to get a

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.439
little bit closer to the ends as a result.

00:02:24.929 --> 00:02:29.525
So let's say if I had my nonane and I
ended up putting it let's say on carbon

00:02:29.525 --> 00:02:31.795
number three, and I have that split.

00:02:31.795 --> 00:02:34.015
Well, how exactly would
you go through naming it?

00:02:34.395 --> 00:02:36.915
You do the exact same
process as before, right?

00:02:36.915 --> 00:02:42.104
We would say that, hey, we have nonane,
which is that nine member, uh, chain.

00:02:42.645 --> 00:02:46.635
And we would end up putting
where that isopropyl is, which

00:02:46.635 --> 00:02:48.234
is at the third position.

00:02:48.684 --> 00:02:50.915
And you would say three isopropyl.

00:02:51.435 --> 00:02:54.395
Nonane, and that's because
of the shape of it, right?

00:02:54.395 --> 00:02:55.355
So that's something else.

00:02:55.465 --> 00:02:59.035
One more thing to really look for is
that whenever you have this special

00:02:59.035 --> 00:03:04.765
shape of this Y shape here That
means that oh you have an isopropyl.

00:03:04.805 --> 00:03:06.044
Now, is that the only one?

00:03:06.055 --> 00:03:10.315
Well, I've kind of saw at the beginning
it was propyls and butyls both

00:03:10.325 --> 00:03:12.620
have them And also Pentels as well.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.840
But we're not going to do
that in this particular video.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.760
Let's take a look at Beutels specifically.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.399
Now when it comes to Beutels, same idea.

00:03:25.499 --> 00:03:28.269
They really need to be in the middle
of the chain because you got to keep in

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:32.140
mind that if you want a Beutel group,
It, it's four carbons long, so it's

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.659
really easy for it to redefine a chain.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.329
So I'm going to put it right here
in the middle, just to kind of

00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:39.449
go through naming this structure.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.309
So butyl is four carbons long.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.469
So if I go through this, we can see
that, all right, I'll name number across.

00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:49.060
So once again, this is also a nonane.

00:03:49.355 --> 00:03:53.305
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

00:03:53.675 --> 00:03:59.494
So nonane, and we can see that our
butyl is at number five in this one.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:02.415
So what we would say is five butyl.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.940
nonane for the name.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.030
Now what kind of shapes do you have?

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:10.200
Well there's other than nonane, other
than butyl, so being that this is a

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:14.650
straight chain butyl, and the same
goes for propyl, is that it's very

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:20.989
common to see the letter n to say
that this is the normal form of butyl.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.849
So whenever you see a little n,
and let's say if you look something

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:27.360
up, there's a reason for that,
that means that it is normal.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:33.200
Now, typically we do not include that,
and it's very uncommon to do that, and

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.809
in fact in my classes I don't ask anybody
to do that because it's not normal to

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.390
do it, even though it's called normal.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.929
So when it comes to the different
shapes of isomers, well, they have

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.289
these little unique shapes to them.

00:04:43.719 --> 00:04:48.219
So the first one, if you kind of look
on the far left here is also ISO.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:50.920
So you can see that we
have the same idea here.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.939
This one kind of reminds me a little bit
of like, almost like a little mermaid

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.420
tail is kind of the way to think about it.

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:02.729
So you have the tail at the end and then
essentially like swimming or maybe like

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.820
a dolphin tail is one way to look at it.

00:05:05.255 --> 00:05:09.465
Now, what makes this one, um, different
than essentially the one that we

00:05:09.465 --> 00:05:15.154
had earlier is the fact that there
is an extra carbon, uh, right here.

00:05:15.154 --> 00:05:19.744
So you can see that we have, uh, going
through these, we have one, two, three,

00:05:20.004 --> 00:05:24.294
four carbons, which if you remember, butyl
is four carbons, and then they have the

00:05:24.325 --> 00:05:26.494
condensed structure here to show as well.

00:05:27.224 --> 00:05:28.965
There's also sec butyl.

00:05:29.205 --> 00:05:33.224
So sec, it's coming from secondary, so
it's sort of like the classification,

00:05:33.224 --> 00:05:37.125
but it's So, with this one, right, you
can see this carbon here in the middle,

00:05:37.505 --> 00:05:39.055
uh, that's connecting the two sides.

00:05:39.055 --> 00:05:41.664
You can see it's uneven as a result.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.445
So it has one, two carbons on each side.

00:05:44.445 --> 00:05:46.075
That's where the secondary comes from.

00:05:46.495 --> 00:05:48.685
And then you have your
fourth carbon as well.

00:05:49.059 --> 00:05:49.969
So be secondary.

00:05:51.409 --> 00:05:53.840
And the last one I always are
kind of referred to as a little

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.739
bit looks like reminds me of like
a chicken foot or a bird foot.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.040
Um, and then with this one, you can
kind of see the three, the three

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.020
little toes that are pointing up.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:07.359
So you have a one, two, three, and
then the fourth one in the middle.

00:06:07.745 --> 00:06:12.185
And that one is tert butyl, so it
has CH3s all around the carbon.

00:06:12.565 --> 00:06:15.715
So that's what we're going to be
seeing right here with that carbon.

00:06:16.205 --> 00:06:19.795
Um, and then each one of them
has its own unique shape to it.

00:06:20.074 --> 00:06:23.055
So let's kind of practice trying
to identify some of these and to

00:06:23.065 --> 00:06:24.755
go through a couple examples of it.

00:06:25.135 --> 00:06:27.985
So that way we, we are familiar
with it when it comes to your class.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:29.020
All right, let's take a look.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.810
So for the following problem, we're
going to write the IUPAC name for

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.570
each of the following molecules.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.170
So let's go through our steps.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.909
The first thing we're going to do is
we're going to identify what our longest,

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.570
uh, carbon chain is or our parent chain.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.769
So with this one, you might be able to
quickly see it that if I highlight across,

00:06:47.325 --> 00:06:50.005
We can see that we have a very long chain.

00:06:50.305 --> 00:06:53.075
So if we end up numbering that,
mean that you want to start the

00:06:53.075 --> 00:06:56.974
numbering, uh, closest to your
substituent or your branch.

00:06:57.384 --> 00:07:03.454
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,
7, and your branch is this

00:07:03.454 --> 00:07:05.895
specific kind of shape over here.

00:07:05.945 --> 00:07:10.104
So if I highlight it, right, we can
see that we have this unique shape.

00:07:10.555 --> 00:07:14.715
Now when it comes to identifying it, the
first thing you got to do is you want to.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.120
Determine how many carbons do
you have, and you can see that

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.020
we have, uh, four carbons.

00:07:20.494 --> 00:07:23.705
In this structure, so we got 1234.

00:07:24.065 --> 00:07:26.305
So that's going to be one
of your beautiful shapes.

00:07:26.675 --> 00:07:30.614
So when we're trying to look at
this, well, I excluded all the shapes

00:07:30.615 --> 00:07:33.585
here and you want to try to play
a little bit of a matching game.

00:07:33.594 --> 00:07:36.905
But ideally, you want to be able to
memorize these because he won't have

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:41.505
maybe these as accessible on, let's say,
an exam or maybe in a lab right away.

00:07:41.795 --> 00:07:43.165
So it's good to know what they are.

00:07:43.664 --> 00:07:47.305
So you'll see that if I look at all the
butyl shapes, the one that kind of sticks

00:07:47.305 --> 00:07:49.585
out the most is actually that sec butyl.

00:07:49.885 --> 00:07:53.875
So the one that's in the middle, and
that's going to be our shape for this one.

00:07:54.315 --> 00:07:55.475
So let's write down our name.

00:07:55.505 --> 00:07:57.734
So the first thing was, right,
you just write down the parent

00:07:57.735 --> 00:07:58.955
chain, and it was seven.

00:07:59.325 --> 00:08:02.115
So that's going to be
heptane is for seven.

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:06.974
And then we have essentially our branch,
which is located at carbon number three.

00:08:07.475 --> 00:08:12.915
So what we're going to simply do is take
three and then we'll say three sec butyl.

00:08:13.375 --> 00:08:18.765
And that would be the name for the sec
butyl heptane would be the entire name.

00:08:19.345 --> 00:08:20.435
Let's try another.

00:08:21.145 --> 00:08:23.455
So if I'm going to look at this
one, well, Oh, we got something

00:08:23.455 --> 00:08:24.365
a little interesting here.

00:08:24.365 --> 00:08:25.665
We have a ring structure.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:27.250
and one of the groups.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:31.219
Well, you might be able to quickly see
that, well, this guy actually quickly

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:36.559
matches the one that's right next to,
which is three carbons, right, is what's

00:08:36.569 --> 00:08:39.169
in this, uh, particular car, this branch.

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:40.709
So it's going to be this isopropyl.

00:08:41.579 --> 00:08:44.100
It's the only unique one for isopropyl.

00:08:44.589 --> 00:08:48.150
So what we're going to do is we're going
to start by writing down isopropyl.

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:52.760
And being that's a ring structure
and it's the only Only branch,

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.219
you do not have to number it.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.030
The only time you do have to
go through numbering is if

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.760
you have multiple branches.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.240
In that case, you want to go via priority.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.859
So whatever's the most important
thing, typically functional

00:09:03.859 --> 00:09:06.549
groups, which is something that'll
be covered in future videos.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.050
So isopropyl, and being that this
is a ring structure, we have to

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.340
count how many carbons we have.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:17.230
We have one, two, three,
four, five, and six.

00:09:17.709 --> 00:09:20.059
So we use the prefix cyclo.

00:09:20.595 --> 00:09:23.965
For a ring and being that it is
six, it's going to be cyclohexane.

00:09:25.165 --> 00:09:29.435
So it's isopropyl cyclohexane
would be the complete name.

00:09:30.145 --> 00:09:30.965
Let's do another.

00:09:31.474 --> 00:09:33.884
So right here, I also have
another one as well, right?

00:09:33.895 --> 00:09:35.564
And the same rules kind of apply.

00:09:35.965 --> 00:09:38.954
You want to find what your
longest carbon chain is.

00:09:39.255 --> 00:09:42.875
So you quickly can see that,
oh, if I go across, I have five.

00:09:43.204 --> 00:09:46.675
Right, and that one's going to be
your longest chain in this case.

00:09:47.105 --> 00:09:50.395
Now there are other options here
that you could have had, right?

00:09:50.395 --> 00:09:52.275
You could go across, that's the easy one.

00:09:52.765 --> 00:09:57.524
Or if you kind of noticed, you could have
actually like wrapped around and done this

00:09:57.525 --> 00:10:01.424
as well as five, and the name is actually
going to be different in this situation.

00:10:02.064 --> 00:10:07.035
Now the thing is, with these specific
shapes, it actually helps simplify names.

00:10:07.365 --> 00:10:11.035
Right, if I pick the one that's like
the dark blue color, I would have

00:10:11.055 --> 00:10:14.005
had three different methyl groups
and it would have been a little

00:10:14.005 --> 00:10:15.695
bit more complicated, possibly.

00:10:16.135 --> 00:10:16.654
Maybe not.

00:10:16.675 --> 00:10:18.795
It kind of depends on
what your preference is.

00:10:19.205 --> 00:10:23.115
But using something that's like a tert
butyl, which is this specific one here,

00:10:23.684 --> 00:10:28.965
it actually allows you to really be
able to sort of consolidate things and

00:10:28.965 --> 00:10:30.695
it can make things a little bit easier.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.350
It's not always, it's personal
preference, but there's not, in this

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:38.830
case there wouldn't be one right or
wrong answer, but you typically do

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.690
see a lot of more favoritism in IUPAC.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:45.330
Towards those special structure
names when it's possible.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.040
So we have five.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.449
So that would be pentane, right?

00:10:49.449 --> 00:10:50.870
So that is what we would use.

00:10:51.309 --> 00:10:54.359
Now we want to alphabetize
our different groups.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.979
So would I go by the T
for tert butyl or not?

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.140
That's really the question.

00:10:59.599 --> 00:11:03.829
Well, being that you want to go by
what the actual branch is, so butyl,

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.479
you go by B, not by the T part of it.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.990
So you always go off of
what the actual branch is.

00:11:10.479 --> 00:11:14.949
So B becomes before M, which is the
methyl that is attached here as well.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:17.030
So just to kind of highlight
where the methyl is.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:25.240
So what we would end up writing is we
would have three T butyl, and then you

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:31.200
have your methyl, Which we have it had in
green there, and then we'd put to methyl.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:36.090
And the only reason we have methyl
coming first is because of alphabetical

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:40.810
order, or sorry, methyl coming
second, uh, butyl comes before.

00:11:41.069 --> 00:11:41.429
Okay?

00:11:42.089 --> 00:11:45.430
Now that's the whole idea of trying
to name these special structures.

